# From static pages to an app

### Routing, load functions and the `tutors.json` contract

Tutors Ladder, week 8. Rung 5 of 7: Reader

---

## Where we are on the ladder

| Rung | Course folder to... | Who renders |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2 Lite | `tutors.json` and one HTML file per page | Node, at build time |
| 3 Layers, 4 Proven | the same, layered, then specified and tested | Node, at build time |
| **5 Reader** | `tutors.json` only | **the browser, when you click** |

For the sample course the cli writes eight pages. The reader ships one page, `200.html`, and a script that
draws whichever page the URL asks for.

Rung 5 is a copy of rung 4. Everything rung 4 proved is still proved: 183 tests there, 409 here.

---

## Who is the customer now?

The **student**, reading a course in a browser. What the student values:

- the right page from any link, typed, bookmarked or shared;
- no waiting between pages;
- a clear answer when a link names nothing;
- always knowing where they are in the course.

Each becomes a requirement this week. Lean calls this **pull**: build what a customer's need asks for, when
it asks, and nothing ahead of it.

---

## Two halves, one file between them

```
course folder ──► apps/cli (gen) ──► tutors.json ──► apps/reader (browser)
                 build time            contract         run time
```

- `pnpm course` runs `node apps/cli/src/main.ts --json-only ...` (R-013) and writes
  `apps/reader/static/courses/ladder-sample-course/tutors.json`.
- The reader `fetch`es that file. It never sees `@ladder/gen` or `node:`.
- The Layering rule says so: `@ladder/reader` may import `@ladder/model` and `@ladder/lite` only.

---

## Why a contract and not a function call

Upstream works the same way (`docs/COURSE-PAGE-WALKTHROUGH.md`):

> This file is the entire course ... The reader never reads a Markdown file from disk. `tutors.json` is
> produced ahead of time by the generator in `packages/jsr/tutors`.

- A lecturer publishes `tutors.json` to any host; one reader shows every course.
- Generator and reader change on their own schedules, as long as the file keeps its shape.
- So the shape is checked from both sides: `tests/conformance/tutors-json.test.ts` compares gen with
  upstream's file; `tests/unit/reader/lo-tree.test.ts` and every reader scenario read **upstream's** file.

---

## Specified first

Rung 4 made tests the specification. So the reader arrived as sentences, before code:

| Id | The reader shall... |
| --- | --- |
| R-014 | fetch a course's `tutors.json` at most once and reuse it |
| R-015, R-016 | treat an odd course id, or an answer that is not a course, as not found |
| R-017 | give the course, each topic, talk, note and lab a page |
| R-018, R-019 | open a lab at its first step; link to the steps either side |
| R-020 | answer 404 with a message that names what is missing |
| R-021 | show breadcrumbs from the course to the page |

Each is a `Rule:` in `tests/bdd/features/reader/reader.feature`. The closure audit holds the two lists together.

---

## SvelteKit maps URLs to folders

```
apps/reader/src/routes/
  +layout.ts                              ssr = false, for every page
  +layout.svelte                          imports app.css
  +page.svelte                            /
  +error.svelte                           the 404 page
  (course-reader)/
    +layout.svelte                        the shell around course pages
    found.ts                              a 404 message becomes error(404, ...)
    course/[courseid]/+page.ts            /course/ladder-sample-course
    topic/[courseid]/[...loid]/+page.ts   /topic/ladder-sample-course/topic-01-basics
    lab/[courseid]/[...loid]/+page.ts     /lab/.../book-1-first-lab/Setup
```

- `[courseid]` is one segment; `[...loid]` is the rest of the path.
- `(course-reader)` groups routes under a layout without adding to the URL.
- Upstream: `apps/reader/src/routes/(course-reader)/`, the same names.

---

## A load function

`apps/reader/src/routes/(course-reader)/topic/[courseid]/[...loid]/+page.ts`

```ts
export const load: PageLoad = async ({ params, url, fetch }) => {
  const { tree, found: topic } = found(await requestLo("topic", params.courseid, url.pathname, fetch));
  currentCourse.value = tree;
  currentLo.value = topic;
  return { topic };
};
```

- Runs before the page renders, on every navigation to it.
- `params.courseid` comes from the folder name `[courseid]`.
- `fetch` is SvelteKit's; the course service takes it as an argument, so a scenario passes its own.
- What it returns arrives in `+page.svelte` as `data`, typed by `./$types`.

---

## A route decides nothing

```ts
// lib/course/course-service.ts
export type PageRequest<T> = { tree: CourseTree; found: T } | { notFound: string };

// routes/(course-reader)/found.ts
export function found<T>(request: PageRequest<T>): { tree: CourseTree; found: T } {
  if ("notFound" in request) error(404, request.notFound);
  return request;
}
```

The course service answers with the page or with the 404 message (R-020). Only `found.ts` speaks SvelteKit.

Why: a scenario and Stryker can reach plain TypeScript without a browser. A rule left inside a route is a
rule nobody proves.

---

## The same load, upstream

`apps/reader/src/routes/(course-reader)/topic/[courseid]/[...loid]/+page.ts` at `2b96b69`:

```ts
export const ssr = false;

export const load: PageLoad = async ({ params, url, fetch }) => {
  const topicId = url.pathname;
  const topic = (await courseService.readTopic(params.courseid, topicId, fetch)) as Topic;
  return { topic: topic };
};
```

Same shape: a route folder, a `courseService` method, an index lookup, one component. One difference worth
noticing: upstream's `readTopic` returns `topic ?? course`, so an unknown topic shows the course page. Rung 5
answers 404 and names the topic (R-020).

---

## One course service, one fetch

`apps/reader/src/lib/course/course-service.ts`

```ts
const courses = new Map<string, CourseTree>();

export async function readCourse(courseId: string, fetch: Fetch): Promise<CourseTree | undefined> {
  const cached = courses.get(courseId);
  if (cached) return cached;                                   // R-014
  const url = courseUrl(courseId);                             // R-015: /^[\w-]+$/ or nothing
  const response = url ? await fetch(url) : undefined;
  // A static host answers a missing file with its fallback page, so "ok" alone does not mean a course.
  const json = response?.ok ? await response.json().catch(() => undefined) : undefined;
  if (!isCourse(json)) return undefined;                       // R-016
  ...
}
```

The Loader rule (`tests/architecture/reader-layers.test.ts`) fails if any other reader file fetches.

---

## Decorating the tree

`tutors.json` routes hold a placeholder: `/topic/{{COURSEURL}}/topic-01-basics`.

`decorateCourseTree` in `src/lib/course/lo-tree.ts` (upstream: `decorateCourseTree` in
`packages/svelte/course/src/course/services/lo-tree.ts`, `injectCourseUrl` in
`packages/jsr/model/src/utils/lo-utils.ts`):

- works on a copy, so the fetched JSON is left as it came;
- replaces `{{COURSEURL}}` with the course id (R-017), and moves the course to `/course/<id>`;
- builds `loIndex`, route to page, in reading order;
- records each object's parent, for breadcrumbs (R-021).

Then a page is one lookup: `findLo(tree, url.pathname)`.

---

## Static, with one fallback page

`apps/reader/vite.config.ts`

```ts
sveltekit({
  adapter: adapter({ pages: "build", assets: "build", fallback: "200.html" }),
}),
```

`src/routes/+layout.ts`: `export const ssr = false;`

- No page is rendered on a server, so the build is a folder of files.
- A host answers every unknown path with `200.html`; the router draws the page in the browser.
- That is why R-016 exists: ask such a host for a missing `tutors.json` and it answers 200, with HTML.
- Upstream: `packages/svelte/app-config/src/svelte.js`, `staticAdapter()` with `STATIC_FALLBACK = '200.html'`.

---

## SvelteKit 3: two changes you will meet

- **Config lives in Vite.** There is no `svelte.config.js`; options go to `sveltekit({...})` in
  `vite.config.ts`. Upstream: `packages/svelte/app-config/src/vite.js`.
- **`#lib`, not `$lib`.** The reader declares `"imports": { "#lib/*": "./src/lib/*" }` in its
  `package.json`, as upstream's `apps/reader/package.json` does.

Both are why examples written for SvelteKit 2 may not compile here. Your agent may have learned from those
examples: `AGENTS.md` says so, and `svelte-check` in the gate catches it.

---

## Which Assertion Ladder rungs guard this week

| Rule | Level | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| every reader requirement is proved | L3/L4 | the closure audit, `pnpm test:ears:audit` |
| one place fetches | L4 | Loader rule |
| `lib/course` is plain TypeScript, without SvelteKit | L4 | Plain logic and Framework rules |
| the reader never imports `gen` or `node:` | L4 | Layering and Browser rules |
| no server code, no storage | L4 | the Rung Fence |
| a route's `data` matches its `load` | L5 | `./$types`, checked by `svelte-check` |
| a route decides nothing | L2, L1 | `AGENTS.md`; five routes as worked examples |

---

## What to take away

- The reader is an app; the generator is a tool; `tutors.json` is the contract between them.
- A URL is a folder; a page is a `load` and a component; a route decides nothing.
- One file fetches; every page is an index lookup in a decorated tree.
- The build is static, with one fallback page, like upstream's static reader.
- The reader was specified first, and so is everything you add: pull, not push.

Next: the upstream reader's routes. Then the lab: specify a route, follow a URL, build the route.
